## Signal Naming and Usage

### Signal Names
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Past tense for events, present for requests
[Signal] public delegate void HealthChangedEventHandler(int newHealth, int oldHealth);
[Signal] public delegate void EnemyDefeatedEventHandler(int enemyId, Vector2 position);
[Signal] public delegate void PhaseEnteredEventHandler(int phaseNumber);
[Signal] public delegate void DamageTakenEventHandler(int amount, Node source);

[Signal] public delegate void ShakeRequestedEventHandler(float intensity, float duration);
[Signal] public delegate void EffectSpawnedEventHandler(Node2D effect, Vector2 position);

// ❌ BAD: Vague names
[Signal] public delegate void ChangedEventHandler(); // What changed?
[Signal] public delegate void EventEventHandler(); // What event?
[Signal] public delegate void OnHitEventHandler(); // Remove "On" prefix (redundant)
```

### Signal Documentation
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Document signals with their parameters
/// <summary>
/// Emitted when the enemy takes damage from any source.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="amount">Damage amount before defense calculations</param>
/// <param name="source">The node that caused the damage (can be null)</param>
/// <param name="isCritical">Whether this was a critical hit</param>
[Signal] public delegate void DamageTakenEventHandler(int amount, Node source, bool isCritical);
```

## Testing and Validation Patterns

### Smoke Test Functions
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Self-test functions for complex systems
private void TestSpawning()
{
    if (!OS.IsDebugBuild())
        return;
    
    GD.Print("[EnemySpawner] Running spawn tests...");
    
    // Test 1: Basic spawn
    var enemy = EntityFactory.SpawnEnemy("popcorn_01", Vector2.Zero, Vector2.Down);
    Debug.Assert(enemy != null, "Basic spawn failed");
    Debug.Assert(IsInstanceValid(enemy), "Spawned enemy is invalid");
    
    // Test 2: Pool exhaustion
    var enemies = new List<Enemy>();
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        var e = EntityFactory.SpawnEnemy("popcorn_01", Vector2.Zero, Vector2.Down);
        if (e != null)
        {
            enemies.Add(e);
        }
    }
    
    GD.Print($"[EnemySpawner] Spawned {enemies.Count} enemies before pool exhaustion");
    
    // Cleanup
    foreach (var e in enemies)
    {
        EntityFactory.ReturnToPool(e, "popcorn_01");
    }
    
    GD.Print("[EnemySpawner] Spawn tests complete");
}
```

### Assertions (Debug Only)
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Use assertions for invariants in debug builds
public int CalculateDamage(int baseDamage, float multiplier)
{
    Debug.Assert(baseDamage >= 0, "Base damage cannot be negative");
    Debug.Assert(multiplier >= 0.0f, "Multiplier cannot be negative");
    
    int result = (int)(baseDamage * multiplier);
    Debug.Assert(result >= 0, "Calculated damage is negative");
    return result;
}

// ⚠️ CAUTION: Assertions are removed in release builds!
// Don't rely on assertions for critical validation
```

## Linting and Quality

### Common Linter Warnings to Fix
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Explicit integer division
int half = count / 2; // Integer division in C#
float ratio = (float)count / 2.0f; // Float division

// ✅ GOOD: Unused variables prefixed with _
public void SomeSignalHandler(int _param1, string _param2)
{
    // Not using parameters, prefix with _
}

// ✅ GOOD: Explicit type cast
var enemy = body as Enemy;
if (enemy != null)
{
    enemy.TakeDamage(10);
}

// ❌ BAD: Unsafe cast without check
var enemy = body as Enemy;
enemy.TakeDamage(10); // Crash if body is not Enemy!
```

## Code Organization Best Practices

### One Class Per File
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: File contains one primary class
// File: EnemyController.cs
public partial class EnemyController : Node2D
{
    // Implementation
}

// ✅ ACCEPTABLE: Small helper class in same file
public class SpawnConfig
{
    public string TemplateId { get; set; }
    public Vector2 Position { get; set; }
    public int Count { get; set; }
}

// ❌ BAD: Multiple unrelated classes in one file
// File: GameStuff.cs
public partial class Enemy : Node2D { }
public partial class Player : Node2D { }
public partial class Bullet : Node2D { }
```

### Constants in Separate Files (When Many)
```csharp
// ✅ GOOD: Large constant sets in dedicated files
// File: GameConstants.cs
public static class GameConstants
{
    public const float PlayerSpeed = 180.0f;
    public const float PlayerFocusedSpeed = 90.0f;
    public const int PlayerMaxPower = 4;
    public const int PlayerStartingLives = 3;
    public const int PlayerStartingBombs = 3;

    public const int BulletMaxCount = 500;
    public const int EnemyMaxCount = 100;
    public const int ParticleMaxCount = 200;
}

// Usage in other files
var speed = GameConstants.PlayerSpeed;
```

## Style Checklist

Before committing:
- [ ] All variables have explicit type annotations
- [ ] All functions have parameter and return types
- [ ] Naming follows conventions (PascalCase public, camelCase private)
- [ ] Private members prefixed with underscore
- [ ] Indentation is consistent (4 spaces)
- [ ] Lines under 120 characters (max 150)
- [ ] Logical grouping with blank lines
- [ ] Early returns used instead of deep nesting
- [ ] Comments explain WHY, not WHAT
- [ ] Signals have descriptive names and typed parameters
- [ ] No linter warnings
- [ ] File structure follows standard order
- [ ] Code matches surrounding style (consistency > personal preference)
- [ ] Use `partial` keyword for all classes
- [ ] Use proper C# syntax and patterns

